	<section class="section testing">
		<div class="wrap"><div class="section-head"><h2>Extensive Ingredient and Finished-Product Testing</h2></div><div class="copy"><p>Quality control begins before a product ever reaches the bottle.</p><p>The manufacturing facility tests incoming ingredients and also evaluates formulations and finished products before release. Testing is designed to help verify ingredient identity, purity, potency and stability throughout the manufacturing process.</p><p>Depending on the ingredient or formula, testing may include:</p><ul class="clean-list testing-list"><li>Identity testing</li><li>Potency or strength testing</li><li>Microbiological testing</li><li>Purity testing</li><li>Heavy-metal analysis</li><li>Pesticide testing</li><li>Allergen testing when applicable</li><li>Stability testing</li><li>Botanical identification</li><li>Visual, microscopic and sensory evaluation</li></ul><p>The exact testing performed varies according to the ingredient, formula and applicable quality specifications.</p></div></div>
	</section>

	<section class="section advanced">
		<div class="wrap"><div class="advanced-intro"><h2>Advanced Testing Meets Traditional Botanical Knowledge</h2><div class="copy"><p>Herbal supplements require more than attractive packaging. Botanical identity, harvesting, handling and extraction methods can all influence the quality of the finished product.</p><p>The manufacturing laboratory uses a combination of traditional botanical evaluation and modern analytical technology to evaluate raw materials and finished products.</p><p>Testing methods may include:</p></div></div><div class="methods">
			<article class="method"><h3>FTIR – Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy</h3><p>Used to help identify chemical compounds and confirm ingredient identity.</p></article>
			<article class="method"><h3>GC – Gas Chromatography</h3><p>Used to evaluate chemical profiles and potency and is commonly used in the analysis of essential oils.</p></article>
			<article class="method"><h3>HP-TLC – High Performance Thin Layer Chromatography</h3><p>Helps identify botanical ingredients, including plant genus and species.</p></article>
			<article class="method"><h3>HPLC – High Performance Liquid Chromatography</h3><p>Used to isolate and measure specific chemical markers and evaluate the potency of certain plant compounds, vitamins and other ingredients.</p></article>
			<article class="method"><h3>ICP-MS – Inductively Coupled Plasma Mass Spectrometry</h3><p>Used to measure and quantify heavy metals in raw materials and finished products.</p></article>
			<article class="method"><h3>Microscopy</h3><p>Examines the cellular structure of whole botanicals to assist with plant identification.</p></article>
			<article class="method"><h3>Macroscopy</h3><p>Uses the visible characteristics of whole plants to help verify botanical genus and species.</p></article>
			<article class="method"><h3>Microbiological Testing</h3><p>Evaluates raw materials and finished products for microorganisms such as aerobic bacteria, yeast, mold, E. coli, Staphylococcus and Salmonella.</p></article>
			<article class="method"><h3>Organoleptic Evaluation</h3><p>Uses traditional botanical characteristics such as taste, color, aroma and appearance as additional quality markers.</p></article>
			<article class="method"><h3>Titration</h3><p>May be used to measure the potency of certain vitamins and minerals.</p></article>
			<article class="method"><h3>UV-VIS – Ultraviolet-Visible Spectroscopy</h3><p>Used to measure concentrations of specific compounds within certain botanical ingredients.</p></article>
		</div><p class="advanced-close">This combination of modern laboratory technology and traditional botanical knowledge gives our manufacturing partner multiple ways to evaluate the identity and quality of the ingredients used in our products.</p></div>
	</section>

	<section class="section safeguards">
